            question: 'Explain the kinked demand curve model of oligopoly pricing.',
            answer: 'Kinked demand curve model:\n\nAssumptions:\n1. If firm raises price, rivals don\'t follow (lose customers)\n2. If firm cuts price, rivals match (no gain in market share)\n3. Creates kink in demand curve at current price P*\n\nDemand curve characteristics:\n- Above P*: Elastic (rivals don\'t follow price increase)\n- Below P*: Inelastic (rivals match price decrease)\n- Kink creates discontinuity in MR curve\n\nImplications:\n1. Price rigidity:\n   - Wide range of costs where P* remains optimal\n   - Changes in MC may not change price\n   - Explains stable prices despite cost variations\n\n2. Non-price competition:\n   - Price wars unprofitable\n   - Firms compete through advertising, quality, innovation\n\n3. Criticism:\n   - Doesn\'t explain how P* determined initially\n   - Oversimplified assumptions about rivals\' behavior\n   - Empirical evidence mixed',
