            question: 'What is collusion and why do oligopolists have incentive to collude?',
            answer: 'Collusion:\n\nDefinition: Agreement among firms to coordinate behavior, typically to restrict output and raise prices\n\nTypes:\n1. Explicit collusion (cartel):\n   - Formal agreement\n   - Coordinate prices, output, market shares\n   - Example: OPEC\n\n2. Tacit collusion:\n   - Informal coordination\n   - No explicit agreement\n   - Price leadership, signaling\n\nIncentive to collude:\n1. Avoid price wars - destructive competition reduces all firms\' profits\n2. Maximize joint profits - act as collective monopoly\n3. Reduce uncertainty - coordinate rather than guess rivals\' actions\n4. Share markets - agree on territories or customers\n\nBenefits:\n- Higher prices and profits\n- Stable market conditions\n- Reduced risk\n\nDrawbacks:\n- Illegal in most countries (antitrust laws)\n- Unstable (cheating incentive)\n- Difficult to coordinate\n- Entry threat',
