            question: 'Explain cartel theory and why cartels are inherently unstable.',
            answer: 'Cartel theory:\n\nCartel formation:\n1. Firms agree to restrict output below competitive level\n2. Raise price toward monopoly price\n3. Allocate production quotas among members\n4. Share monopoly profits\n\nWhy cartels are unstable:\n\n1. Cheating incentive:\n   - Each firm benefits from others restricting output\n   - Individual firm can increase profit by secretly exceeding quota\n   - At cartel price P*, MR > MC for each firm\n   - Prisoner\'s dilemma situation\n\n2. Detection difficulties:\n   - Hard to monitor compliance\n   - Secret price cuts, quality improvements\n   - Delayed discovery of cheating\n\n3. Entry threat:\n   - High prices attract new entrants\n   - If barriers weak, cartel profits eroded\n\n4. Coordination problems:\n   - Disagreements over quota allocation\n   - Different cost structures\n   - Conflicting interests\n\n5. Legal restrictions:\n   - Cartels illegal in most jurisdictions\n   - Risk of prosecution deters formation\n\nSuccessful cartels rare and temporary.',
