            question: 'What is limit pricing and how does it act as a barrier to entry?',
            answer: 'Limit pricing:\n\nDefinition: Incumbent firms set price below profit-maximizing level to deter entry\n\nStrategy:\n1. Identify potential entrant\'s cost structure\n2. Set price below entrant\'s AC but above own AC\n3. Entry becomes unprofitable for potential competitor\n4. Incumbent sacrifices short-run profit for long-run market position\n\nConditions for effectiveness:\n1. Incumbent has cost advantage (economies of scale, experience)\n2. Credible commitment to maintain low price post-entry\n3. Entry requires significant sunk costs\n4. Incumbent can sustain period of lower profits\n\nMechanism:\n- Price < P_monopoly but P > AC_incumbent\n- P < AC_entrant\n- Entrant expects losses if enters\n- Deters entry, preserving incumbent\'s market power\n\nTrade-off:\n- Lower short-run profits\n- Avoid competition and protect long-run profits\n- Maintain market power\n\nCriticism:\n- May not be credible (why not raise price after entry deterred?)\n- Entrant may enter anyway if expects incumbent to accommodate',
