            question: 'Explain predatory pricing and evaluate its effectiveness as a competitive strategy.',
            answer: 'Predatory pricing:\n\nDefinition: Setting price below cost to drive competitors out of market, then raising price once rivals exit\n\nStrategy:\n1. Phase 1: Price below AC (or even AVC)\n2. Incumbent incurs losses but has deeper pockets\n3. Rivals cannot sustain losses and exit\n4. Phase 2: Raise price to recoup losses once competition eliminated\n\nRequirements:\n1. Financial strength to sustain losses\n2. Rivals are financially weaker\n3. Barriers prevent re-entry after price increase\n4. Can recoup losses with higher future profits\n\nProblems with strategy:\n\n1. Costly:\n   - Incumbent suffers losses during predatory phase\n   - May not recover losses if competition returns\n\n2. Difficult to sustain:\n   - How long can maintain below-cost prices?\n   - Rivals may survive longer than expected\n\n3. Entry threat:\n   - After raising price, new entrants may enter\n   - Hit-and-run entry possible\n   - Must repeat predation\n\n4. Legal issues:\n   - Illegal under competition law in many countries\n   - Antitrust prosecution risk\n\n5. Reputation effects:\n   - May deter future entry (benefit)\n   - But creates hostile regulatory environment\n\nEffectiveness debated: Rarely successful in practice.',
