            question: 'Explain the basic concepts of game theory as applied to oligopoly, using the Prisoner\'s Dilemma.',
            answer: 'Game theory in oligopoly:\n\nKey concepts:\n1. Players - firms making strategic decisions\n2. Strategies - possible actions (price, output, advertising)\n3. Payoffs - profits resulting from strategy combinations\n4. Nash equilibrium - outcome where no firm can improve by changing strategy alone\n\nPrisoner\'s Dilemma applied to oligopoly:\n\nScenario: Two firms deciding whether to collude (cooperate) or compete\n\nPayoff matrix example:\n                Firm B: Collude    Firm B: Compete\nFirm A: Collude     $10m, $10m        $2m, $15m\nFirm A: Compete     $15m, $2m         $5m, $5m\n\nAnalysis:\n1. Joint profit maximized if both collude ($10m each)\n2. Dominant strategy: Compete (better regardless of rival\'s choice)\n3. Nash equilibrium: Both compete ($5m each)\n4. Suboptimal outcome: Could earn more by colluding\n\nImplications:\n1. Individual rationality leads to collective irrationality\n2. Explains instability of cartels\n3. Why explicit agreements needed (but illegal)\n4. Repeated games may allow cooperation\n\nApplications: Price wars, advertising races, capacity expansion',
