            question: 'Distinguish between dominant strategy and Nash equilibrium in game theory.',
            answer: 'Dominant strategy:\n\nDefinition: A strategy that yields highest payoff regardless of what rivals do\n\nCharacteristics:\n1. Best response to all possible rival strategies\n2. Player should always choose dominant strategy\n3. Simplifies decision-making (no need to predict rivals)\n4. Not all games have dominant strategies\n\nExample (Prisoner\'s Dilemma):\n- "Compete" is dominant strategy for both firms\n- Yields higher profit whether rival colludes or competes\n\nNash equilibrium:\n\nDefinition: Outcome where each player\'s strategy is best response to others\' strategies - no player can improve by unilaterally changing\n\nCharacteristics:\n1. Mutual best response\n2. Stable outcome (no incentive to deviate)\n3. Requires predicting rivals\' choices\n4. May not be unique or exist\n\nRelationship:\n1. If all players have dominant strategies, their combination is Nash equilibrium\n2. Nash equilibrium can exist without dominant strategies\n3. Dominant strategy Nash equilibrium is strongest solution concept\n\nExample without dominant strategy:\n- Coordination games (e.g., technology standards)\n- Multiple Nash equilibria possible\n- Players must coordinate to reach efficient outcome',
