            answer: 'Diversification strategy:\n\nDefinition: Expanding into new products or markets beyond core business\n\nTypes:\n1. Related diversification - similar products or markets\n2. Unrelated diversification - different industries\n\nBenefits:\n\n1. Risk reduction:\n   - Spread across multiple markets\n   - Reduce dependence on single product/market\n   - Counter cyclical variations\n\n2. Growth opportunities:\n   - Saturated core market\n   - Leverage existing capabilities\n   - Cross-selling opportunities\n\n3. Economies of scope:\n   - Share resources across products\n   - Common distribution, marketing, R&D\n   - Synergies between divisions\n\n4. Competitive advantage:\n   - Bundle products\n   - One-stop shopping for customers\n   - Cross-subsidization possible\n\nRisks:\n\n1. Loss of focus:\n   - Dilute core competencies\n   - Complexity of managing diverse businesses\n   - Resource allocation challenges\n\n2. Diseconomies of scale:\n   - Coordination costs\n   - Bureaucracy\n   - Reduced efficiency\n\n3. Unsuccessful ventures:\n   - Unfamiliar markets\n   - Lack of expertise\n   - Cultural integration problems\n\n4. Regulatory issues:\n   - Antitrust concerns\n   - Conglomerate discount in stock market\n\nExamples: Samsung (electronics to construction), GE (multiple industries)',
